1% y/y in Q4 2025 and +1.8% in Q1 2026, then fell 4% in Q2 2026. That was the first decline in three years.\n- Voltaris shipments fell 9% in Q2 2026, the steepest drop among large makers. Its share of Caldrian transformer shipments fell from 31% to 27%, with Tarnwell and imports gaining.\n- Shipments typically trail orders by about two quarters, so the Q2 weakness may carry into the second half of 2026 and beyond.\n- Voltaris quarterly revenue was C$905M (Q3 2025), C$960M (Q4 2025), C$980M (Q1 2026) and C$890M (Q2 2026). The last four quarters sum to C$3.735B.\n\n**Implied 2027 revenue**\n- Guidance of C$4.2B is about 12% above the trailing-four-quarter revenue of C$3.735B. Reaching it would need an annual run rate of about C$1.05B per quarter, against C$890M in Q2.\n- If share stays near 27% and industry shipments are flat to slightly down, revenue stays around C$3.5–3.7B. That assumes revenue moves roughly in line with shipments.\n- A rebound to 31% share would lift that by about 15% (31/27), to roughly C$4.0–4.2B. Nothing in the data suggests a share recovery.\n- If the share loss continues, revenue could fall to about C$3.3B or lower.\n\n**Caveats**\n- I did not factor in price increases, mix shift, or non-transformer segments, because the data I found doesn't cover them. These could add some revenue. Voltaris's orders and backlog, covered by n2, would show whether they do.\n- Shipments are in units and revenue is in dollars, so the link between them is approximate.","ttft_ms":2920,"type":"result","duration_ms":25024,"uuid":"afe070b3-a94c-42f5-821c-957e31b7605f","ttft_stream_ms":1494,"time_to_request_ms":120,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":11,"system_prompt":11,"resume_other":0,"process_user_input":4,"input_attachments":22,"input_hooks":7,"conversation_append":3,"transcript_persist":4,"tool_pool_refresh":0,"init_message":15,"init_frame_send":5,"engine_pickup":1,"loop_entry":6,"message_prep":0,"model_config_wait":1,"autocompact":1,"request_announcements":7,"